  "Question": "If \\(a\\gt1,\\) \\(b\\gt1\\) and \\(p=\\frac{\\log_b(\\log_ba)}{\\log_ba},\\) then \\(a^p\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(b\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\log_ab\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\log_ba\\)",
    "E": "\\(a^{\\log_ba}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The expression \\(\\log_b a\\) appears twice, so let\n\\[\nt=\\log_b a.\n\\]\nSince \\(a>1\\) and \\(b>1\\), we have \\(t>0\\). Thus \\(\\log_b t\\) is defined and division by \\(t\\) is allowed. The definition of a logarithm gives \\(a=b^t\\), while the given expression becomes \\(p=(\\log_b t)/t\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\na^p\n&=(b^t)^{\\frac{\\log_b t}{t}}\\\\\n&=b^{\\log_b t}\\\\\n&=t.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe last equality follows because exponentiation with base \\(b\\) reverses taking a logarithm with base \\(b\\). Hence \\(\\boxed{a^p=\\log_b a}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
